<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Longhouse Religion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1799</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Code of Handsome Lake, Gaiwiio</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Longhouse Religion is a Haudenosaunee faith founded by the Seneca prophet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/handsome-lake">Handsome Lake</a> after his visions of 1799. It combines the traditional cycle of thanksgiving ceremonies with a moral code, the Gaiwiio, that condemned alcohol and witchcraft and stressed family life. It is still practised in longhouses across Iroquois communities in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/longhouse-religion.jpg" alt="Longhouse Religion" width="320" height="271" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arthur C. Parker (Life time: (1881 – 1955)) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Onondaga_Longhouse.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
